    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Seville grew from a Tartessian settlement into the Roman city of Hispalis, a Visigothic royal residence at times, the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> until 716, the capital of the Abbadid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> from 1023 and a twin capital of the Almohad empire from 1163.</li><li>Ferdinand III took it after a siege of fifteen months in 1248, and from 1503 its port held the monopoly of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish Americas</a>, which brought it to more than a hundred thousand people in the 16th century.</li><li>Silting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> and the plague of 1649 began a decline, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> went to Cádiz in 1717, and the city later hosted the Ibero-American Exposition of 1929 and Expo '92.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Seville's motto NO8DO is a rebus read as the Spanish &quot;No me ha dejado&quot;, &quot;She has not abandoned me&quot;, with a figure eight standing for madeja, a skein of wool; according to legend Alfonso X gave it to the city after its people stayed loyal to him against his son.</p></aside>
    </section>

