    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The original core of the town is dated to this century, when it stood on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">712</span><span class="rf-text">Seville is taken for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and serves as the seat of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> until 716</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1023</span><span class="rf-text">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdom ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadid dynasty</a> emerges with its capital at Seville</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1163</span><span class="rf-text">Seville becomes the seat of the Andalusi part of the Almohad empire, a twin capital with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1248</span><span class="rf-text">The city surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> after fifteen months of siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1503</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> is placed at Seville, which becomes the port of the Indies trade</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1649</span><span class="rf-text">A great plague, worsened by flooding, halves the population</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text">The Ibero-American Exposition opens</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Seville stands on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> in the south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, in a fertile valley where the average height above sea level is 7 metres. The original core of the town, in the neighbourhood of the present Cuesta del Rosario, dates to the 8th century BCE, when it stood on an island in the river. Excavations in 1999 found human remains under the north wall of the Real Alcázar dating to the 8th or 7th century BCE. The town was called Spal or Ispal by the Tartessians, the pre-Roman people who controlled the Guadalquivir valley and traded with the neighbouring Phoenician colonies on the coast. The name Hisbaal is also recorded, and one proposal links it to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>.</p><p>By myth the founder was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules</a>, commonly identified with the Phoenician god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melqart">Melqart</a>, who was said to have sailed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-gibraltar">Strait of Gibraltar</a> and founded trading posts at the sites of Cádiz and Seville. One derivation takes the name Sevilla from the Phoenician word for "lowland" or "plain". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> gained dominance in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> in the 6th century BCE, and later Greek sources blamed their assaults for the destruction of Tartessos, which had collapsed by 530 BCE, either by armed conflict or by the cutting off of Greek trade. The Tartessians were succeeded by the Turdetani and the Turduli.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Seville&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology and derivatives, Early periods, Early modern period:</p><ul><li>Elizabeth Nash, Seville, Cordoba, and Granada: A Cultural History: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, USA, 2005)</li><li>Elizabeth Nash, Seville, Cordoba, and Granada: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giralda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giralda.jpg" alt="Giralda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sevilla_Cathedral_-_Giralda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ingo Mehling, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the city was Hispalis. It developed into one of the great market and industrial centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, while the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italica">Italica</a>, founded in 206 or 205 BCE and the birthplace of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian, remained a residential city. Hispalis had walls and a forum, and was a commercial port on the river the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Baetis</a>. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausonius">Ausonius</a> ranked it tenth among the most important cities of the empire. Under Muslim rule it was reportedly depopulated for a time after the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> moved briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1162. After Seville became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>' seat in 1163 a large urban renewal followed, and by the end of the 12th century the walled city perhaps held 80,000 people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a> was raised between 1184 and 1198 and the Torre del Oro built around 1200.</p><p>The greatest age came after 1503, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> made Seville the gateway of the Indies. As the only port with the royal monopoly of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish Americas</a>, it drew merchants from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, who had to come to Seville for American goods. Its factories in Triana made soap, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and ceramics, and the population grew to more than a hundred thousand, making it the largest and most urbanised city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at the time. The Royal Mint of 1585 to 1587 turned American gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> into coin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gunderic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunderic.jpg" alt="Gunderic" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gunderic">Gunderic</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gunderico_no_Corpus_Pelagianum_(BNE_Mss_1513).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> held Hispalis from the 5th century after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunderic">Gunderic</a> in 426 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechila">Rechila</a> in 441, and the royal residence was moved there under Amalaric, Theudis and Theudigisel before it was fixed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. In 585 the king's son Hermenegild, newly converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, rebelled in the city against his father Leuvigild and was executed. Seville was taken for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> in 712 or 713, and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> sat there until 716, when the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of Córdoba an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadid dynasty</a> was based in Seville from 1023 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> conquered it in 1091. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> took it in the mid-12th century, and in 1163 it became the definitive seat of their Andalusi lands, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> laid siege to the city in 1247 and it surrendered on 23 November 1248. He died in the Alcázar in 1252 and was buried in the cathedral. His son Alfonso X kept Seville as one of the kingdom's capitals, and the court, which had no permanent seat, most often resided there. The Catholic Monarchs placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> in the city in 1503, and the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> administration moved it to Cádiz in 1717. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> held its first tribunal at Seville in 1478.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Seville&#8221; cites where it covers Early periods, Early modern period:</p><ul><li>Elizabeth Nash, Seville, Cordoba, and Granada: A Cultural History: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, USA, 2005)</li><li>Elizabeth Nash, Seville, Cordoba, and Granada: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion.jpg" alt="Casa de Contratación" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Real_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Sevilla,_Sevilla_(42052074324).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FlyingCrimsonPig, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Umayyad rule most of the population were Muladi converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, with Christian and Jewish minorities, and the city remained the see of a Metropolitan Archbishop until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> arrived in the 12th century. The Ibn Adabbas Mosque, built in 830 as the main congregational mosque, later became the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> period Seville was an important scholarly and literary centre. After the Castilian conquest churches were built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> and Gothic styles, and the cathedral rose on the site of the great mosque, begun after 1401 and finished in 1506. Alfonso X founded general schools of Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in 1254, and the present University of Seville dates its foundation to 1505.</p><p>In the 16th century the city had consulates of European governments and merchants from across the continent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> trained pilots and taught cosmography and mathematics. The Seville of Cervantes, who lived there between 1596 and 1600, is the setting of his Rinconete y Cortadillo. In the 17th century the city became one of convents, with 45 monasteries and 28 convents for women by 1671. Murillo, Zurbarán, Valdés Leal and the sculptor Martínez Montañés worked there, and Velázquez was born in the city. The Holy Week processions, organised by lay brotherhoods, took their form in the same period. Seville's first newspaper outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> appeared in 1758.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Seville&#8221; cites where it covers Theatres, Music, Gastronomy:</p><ul><li>Elizabeth Nash, Seville, Cordoba, and Granada:A Cultural History: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sailed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> in 844 and took Seville by storm. They inflicted many casualties and took hostages for ransom, and the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, Abd ar-Rahman II, met them in a battle at Tablada, where about a thousand Vikings fell and four hundred were captured and executed. In the 14th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 and an earthquake in 1356 struck the city, and in 1391 it was the starting point of anti-Jewish massacres that spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon. About 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were killed, many others were forced to convert, all the synagogues were seized and made into churches, and the Jewish quarter virtually disappeared. The first auto de fe of the Inquisition took place in Seville on 6 February 1481, when six people were burned alive.</p><p>In the 17th century silting of the Guadalquivir made the port harder to use, and the great plague of 1649, worsened by flooding, may have killed some 60,000 of about 130,000 people. The population did not recover until the early 19th century. Yellow fever in 1800 wiped out a third of the population, and French troops under Marshal Victor occupied the city from February 1810 until 27 August 1812. At the start of the Civil War General Queipo de Llano took the city in July 1936, resistance in the working-class quarters was crushed, and repression until January 1937 killed 3,028 people. A flood of the Tamarguillo in November 1961 inundated whole neighbourhoods.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Alcázar, the cathedral and the General Archive of the Indies form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in the old town. The Alcázar began in the 10th century as a governor's palace, was enlarged under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadids</a> and rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> style under Pedro I in the 1360s. The cathedral incorporates the minaret of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> mosque as its bell tower, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a>, raised to about 95 metres by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> belfry. The Archive, designed by Juan Herrera in 1572 for the merchants' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, became the archive of the Indies in 1785 and holds documents on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. The Royal Tobacco Factory, built between 1728 and 1771, is now the seat of the university's rectorate.</p><p>The Ibero-American Exposition of 1929 left the María Luisa Park and the Plaza de España, and Expo '92 left a technology park on the island of La Cartuja. The city is also known for its operas: a study of 2012 counted 153 set there, among them Mozart's The Marriage of Figaro, Rossini's The Barber of Seville and Bizet's Carmen. Seville is today the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, with a municipal population of about 688,714 and a river port that is the only one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Seville&#8221; cites where it covers Early modern period, Late modern history, Civic buildings and other monuments:</p><ul><li>Elizabeth Nash, Seville, Cordoba, and Granada: A Cultural History (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Seville, on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, grew from a Tartessian settlement into Roman Hispalis, an Abbadid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> capital and, after 1503, the port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s American trade. Silting and plague ended its monopoly, and it is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>.</em></p>

